2.34.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
vmcb_mark_dirty(to_vmcb, VMCB_LBR);
Reason:
svm_copy_lbrs() unconditionally called vmcb_mark_dirty(VMCB_LBR) on the destination VMCB. When this function was called with vmcb12 as the destination during nested VM exit (nested_svm_vmexit()), it incorrectly cleared the clean bit for VMCB_LBR in vmcb12, which is not architecturally defined and caused a bug.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
void svm_copy_lbrs(struct vmcb *to_vmcb, struct vmcb *from_vmcb)
{
	to_vmcb->save.dbgctl		= from_vmcb->save.dbgctl;
	to_vmcb->save.br_from		= from_vmcb->save.br_from;
	to_vmcb->save.br_to		= from_vmcb->save.br_to;
	to_vmcb->save.last_excp_from	= from_vmcb->save.last_excp_from;
	to_vmcb->save.last_excp_to	= from_vmcb->save.last_excp_to;
}
Reason:
Removing the vmcb_mark_dirty() call from svm_copy_lbrs() avoids the unconditional marking of VMCB_LBR dirty. Callers are now responsible for marking the destination VMCB dirty as appropriate. In particular, for nested VM exit to vmcb12, no marking is done, preserving the clean bits as architecturally required.

